-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Cache Replacement (2Q)
+
+The cache is replaced by the simplified 2Q policy: two resident segments share the single 12 GiB budget.
+
+- **A1** — recent first-use entries, ordered first-in-first-out.
+- **Am** — reused entries, ordered least-recently-used.
+
+A1 is held to a target share of the budget, `A1_TARGET_FRACTION`, with Am holding the remainder. Both segments store rendered JPEG bytes; the split is a target measured in bytes against the shared 12 GiB, not a second disk budget.
+
+**On admission** (a render completes and the sheet is not already resident): the sheet enters A1 as its most-recent entry.
+
+**On a cache hit:**
+
+- If the entry is in A1, move it to Am as Am's most-recent entry. A second touch is what marks an entry reused and promotes it out of scan-exposed A1.
+- If the entry is in Am, move it to Am's most-recent position (LRU touch).
+
+**On eviction** (admitting a sheet would exceed 12 GiB): while the cache is over budget, if A1's resident bytes exceed `A1_TARGET_FRACTION` of the budget, evict A1's oldest entry; otherwise evict Am's least-recently-used entry. A long single-touch screening therefore churns within A1 and leaves Am's reused comparison set resident.
+
+The external interface, the FIFO render queue and its four-job concurrency, the cache key, deduplication, and the 12 GiB budget are all unchanged by this policy. All 2Q segments, ordering, and the size split are private to the worker; no other node observes them.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache replacement policy | Simplified 2Q (A1 first-use + Am reused) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q keeps a reused comparison set resident during long single-touch screenings, the workload the worker must protect. LRU is the cheapest but a sequential screening evicts the comparison set. Frequency-with-aging retains popular clips but adds per-entry counter maintenance and lags a project switch. 2Q's cost — two segments and one size-split parameter — was accepted to protect comparison sheets during big screenings. |
+| 2Q variant | Simplified 2Q: A1 resident + Am, one size parameter | Full 2Q with an A1out ghost queue of evicted keys | A miss re-renders in ~450 ms rather than faulting from a backing store, so a non-resident ghost queue buys little; the simplified variant keeps bookkeeping to one resident split and one parameter. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. `A1_TARGET_FRACTION` value. A smaller A1 protects the reused set harder but admits fewer first-use entries before they can earn a second touch; a larger A1 adapts faster to a new working set but lets a screening reach deeper toward Am. Proposed default: 25% of the 12 GiB budget (3 GiB). Tunable without interface or spec change.
